 # Registry Design
 
-## Context
+## Context and Design Philosophy
 
 Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot contains newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest.
 
+The registry is fully offline: lookups are answered from a local embedded SQLite index on the kiosk disk, and the only update channel is a signed USB snapshot. Lookups must be predictable and complete within 250 ms on a kiosk with 4 GB of RAM, holding roughly 90,000 records.
+
 ## Architecture
 
 Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, index building, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is a local embedded SQLite database: one active index file on the kiosk disk, rebuilt in full whenever a validated snapshot is activated. The active index file is the only lookup source; there is no network path.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
 Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
 
+Validation streams the snapshot rather than loading it whole: a 150 MB snapshot must import on a 4 GB kiosk without excessive memory use.
+
+## Index Build and Activation
+
+Build the new index into a fresh SQLite file beside the active one, inserting all records in a single transaction. Each record stores its fields verbatim plus a normalized lookup key — surrounding whitespace stripped, ASCII letters uppercased — and the unique index enforces uniqueness over that normalized key, so the uniqueness check during validation and the lookup path agree on one normalization. The snapshot date is stored in the index file, so every result is answered from a single self-describing source.
+
+Activate the new snapshot by atomically replacing the active index file only after the build transaction commits. Until then, lookups continue against the previous active index. The previous index file is retained until activation completes, so a failed or interrupted build leaves the last valid snapshot available.
+
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup, then query the active index on the normalized key. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | The HLD tenet forbidding runtime network access rules out both networked options for lookups. SQLite gives predictable offline lookups within the 250 ms goal with no service to run, no credentials, and no lab-network dependency. The accepted cost: each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index when a snapshot arrives — acceptable because imports are infrequent, and all of that stays inside this node. |
+| Rebuild strategy | Full rebuild into a fresh index file, atomic swap on commit | In-place update of the existing index | A fresh-file build keeps the active index untouched and serving during the rebuild, makes "no partially imported snapshot visible" structural, and makes failure recovery trivial: delete the incomplete file and keep the previous snapshot. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Recovery behavior when the active index file is found corrupt or missing at startup (e.g., rebuild from a retained copy of the last snapshot, or require re-import from USB).
+2. Whether to retain the previous index file after activation as a rollback artifact, and for how long.
+3. Import duration budget: how long a full rebuild of 90,000 records may take before it inconveniences the importing technician.
+
